+# Block placement performance review
+
+Reviewed: `src/ServerScriptService/BlockPlacement.server.luau` and
+`src/StarterPlayer/StarterPlayerScripts/BlockPlacer.luau`, against six suggested
+optimizations.
+
+## Summary
+
+There is no performance problem to fix. Nobody has reported lag, nothing has been
+measured as slow, and an estimate of the worst case puts the whole system well
+under 1% of any budget. Five of the six suggestions are declined. One (item 5) is
+made, as a simplification rather than for speed.
+
+## The load, worst case
+
+- **Rate:** the server rejects placements less than 0.1 s apart per player, so at
+  most 10 per second per player. With 8 players that is **80 placements per
+  second per server**, and in practice far fewer, since nobody clicks 10 times a
+  second for long.
+- **Server CPU:** creating, configuring, and parenting one `Part` costs about 15
+  to 20 µs. 80 of them is about 1.6 ms per second, or roughly **0.03 ms per
+  frame** at 60 Hz: about 0.2% of a 16.7 ms frame. The validation around it
+  (type checks, a dictionary lookup, a distance check) is nanoseconds.
+- **Network, client to server:** one call carries a short string and a
+  `Vector3`, about 35 to 40 bytes. At 10 per second that is **under 0.4 KB/s per
+  client**.
+- **Client CPU:** one raycast per click, at human click rates.
+
+The real long-term cost of this system is not in either script: it is the number
+of permanent blocks that pile up in `Workspace` over a long session (instance
+memory, replication on join, physics and render bookkeeping). None of the six
+suggestions touch that. If it ever matters, measure `Stats.InstanceCount`, join
+time, and client memory on a server that has been running for hours, and consider
+a block cap, streaming, or merging blocks.
+
+## Verdicts
+
+### 1. Pool the block Parts: declined
+
+A pool saves work only when objects are destroyed and created again. Placed blocks
+are never removed: there is no code path that destroys one, so nothing would ever
+return to the pool. A pool here would just be a stock of extra parts created
+up front, which costs the same `Instance.new` calls earlier plus idle memory (0.6
+to 6 KB per part).
+
+Even with removal, pooling is worth it at hundreds of creations per second, or
+when a profile shows creation in the frame. Here it is at most 80 per second
+across the server (about 0.03 ms per frame). A server-side pool would also add to
+what every joining player has to download, and reset code is a lasting source of
+stale-state bugs (a reused block keeping the last block's attribute or material).
+
+### 2. Pack PlaceBlock's arguments into a buffer: declined
+
+Buffers save bytes, not time. Measured payloads: a `Vector3` argument is about 22
+bytes per call; a three-field record went from 36 bytes as plain arguments to 28
+as a buffer. Packing this call would save perhaps 10 to 15 bytes, so about 0.1
+KB/s per client at the maximum rate, on a connection that handles hundreds of
+times that. In exchange the server would have to decode and validate a raw buffer
+(length checks, bounds checks, a block-type index) instead of the clear `typeof`
+checks it has now, and both scripts would need a shared encoding kept in sync.
+
+### 3. Make PlaceBlock an UnreliableRemoteEvent: declined
+
+This would change what the game does, not just how fast. Unreliable events may be
+dropped or arrive out of order, so some clicks would silently place nothing.
+`UnreliableRemoteEvent` is meant for continuous, replaceable data such as
+positions and aim, where a lost update is overwritten by the next one. A block
+placement is a one-off action that must arrive, which is what `RemoteEvent` is
+for. "Faster" also does not apply: the only gain from unreliable delivery is not
+waiting behind a lost packet, and at a few dozen bytes per click that is not a
+problem anyone has seen.
+
+### 4. Add `--!native` to both scripts: declined
+
+Native code generation speeds up Luau number crunching: tight math loops,
+buffer processing. Both scripts mostly call engine APIs (`Instance.new`, property
+sets, `Raycast`, `FireServer`), which native compilation does nothing for, and
+they run on clicks, not every frame. It also costs compile time and memory from a
+limit shared by the whole game. The rule is to put `@native` on the few functions
+a profile names, and no profile names these.
+
+### 5. Replace the `table.find` check with a lookup table: made, for simplicity
+
+The speed difference is irrelevant: `table.find` over 5 names is a handful of
+string comparisons, nanoseconds per click. (Sets win by 70x over 1,000 items, not
+5.)
+
+But the change is still worth making because the lookup table already existed.
+`BlockTypes.ByName` was being read on the very next line, and `VALID_NAMES` was
+a second copy of the same information built just for the check. The server now
+looks the name up in `BlockTypes.ByName` once and returns early when it is
+missing, and `VALID_NAMES` is gone. Same behavior (unknown names are still
+ignored), four fewer lines, one source of truth.
+
+### 6. Create the RaycastParams once: declined
+
+`RaycastParams.new()` runs once per click, at human click rates. Its cost is
+nowhere near measurable. Caching it is also not free in correctness: the filter
+holds the character, and the character is replaced on every respawn, so a cached
+params object would need a `CharacterAdded` hook (or a refresh before each cast)
+to avoid filtering a destroyed model. The current code builds the right filter
+every time with no extra state. It is the simpler version and stays.
+
+## Change made
+
+`src/ServerScriptService/BlockPlacement.server.luau`: removed `VALID_NAMES` and
+the `table.find` check; the handler validates the block type by looking it up in
+`BlockTypes.ByName` and returning when the result is `nil`.
+
+## When to look again
+
+Revisit performance if any of these show up: reports of lag or stutter when
+building, a MicroProfiler capture with block creation in a spike frame, slow joins
+on long-running servers, or a design change such as larger servers, bulk placement
+(fill tools, pasting structures), or blocks that can be removed and re-placed
+often. Measure first, then pick the fix that the measurement points at.
